Kubernetes Deployment File – Let’s Understand it Line by Line 

Interviewer:
 Can you explain what happens in a deployment.yaml file?


Sure! A deployment.yaml file is a set of instructions to Kubernetes, saying:
I want to run this app. I need this many pods. Here's the image to use. This is the port it listens on. If anything crashes, restart it automatically.

Let’s break it block-by-block

apiVersion: apps/v1
This tells Kubernetes:
        Which version of the Deployment API are we using?
        (apps/v1 is standard for deployments)


kind: Deployment
This defines the type of Kubernetes object we’re creating.
Here, it’s a Deployment — which manages and maintains your application.


metadata:
      name: my-app
Gives the deployment a name — helpful for identifying it during updates, scaling, or deletion.


spec:
The core of your deployment config.
      replicas: 2
Run 2 pods of this app — for high availability.
Even if one crashes, the other keeps running.


selector: matchLabels:
    app: my-app
This connects the deployment to pods —
"Manage only the pods that have this label."

template:
This is the blueprint for pods that this deployment will create.
labels:
    app: my-app
Add this label to each pod so it can be tracked.


spec.containers:
The section that defines the container inside the pod:
    - name: my-app
       image: my-app:latest
       ports:
             - containerPort: 8000


image:
Tells Kubernetes which Docker image to run.

containerPort:
Defines the port inside the container where the app listens.


Real-World Thinking:
When you apply this file using:
          kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml

Kubernetes will:
Create 2 pods (as per replicas)
Pull the image
Start the containers
Restart them if they crash
Ensure everything matches the defined desired state



YAML file Kubernetes ko ek instruction manual deta hai — ki bhai app ko kaise chalana hai, kitni baar chalana hai, aur agar kuch fail ho gaya toh kaise theek karna hai.




Interview Summary Answer:
A deployment.yaml defines the desired state for an application in Kubernetes — including how many pods to run, what image to use, what labels to apply, and how to connect everything together. Kubernetes takes care of maintaining that state automatically.
